;;; `forced-2.ein` with the walk's forced-positive cascade off — **W1** at layer
;;; 0, reached once W2's mechanism is out of the way. Regression
;;; `forced-2-off` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; Below this header the program is `forced-2.ein`'s: Ein has no flag that
;;; turns the cascade off. The answer by hand is the same: **`Solution`, k = 1,
;;; a model with no `q` fact** — root is consistent and its one hypothesis dies.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered before `fbcae3b2` ─────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   verdict         No solution — the constraints are contradictory
;;;   unsat core (1 facts)   (q one)
;;;   exhausted        true
;;;   enterings        1 (dead_post=1)
;;;
;;; With no cascade, `(q one)` was entered at layer 1 and died. Every layer-1
;;; singleton was dead, so `phase2` broke on an empty `a_layer` without asking
;;; whether root was then complete, and `finalise` unioned the dead cores into a
;;; `Contradiction` — the core is a *hypothesis*, not a program fact.
;;;
;;; Now R is {∅}, root alone (`ein solve FILE`), as `forced-2.ein`'s. No config
;;; option changes R (Q-M1e2.30); the answer above is the one the walk once
;;; gave.
;;;
;;;
;;;
;;;
(relation thing T :why "{?1} is a thing")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")
(relation bad T :why "{?1} is bad")
(thing one)
(rule taint () :match (q ?x)   :assert (bad ?x) :why "q holds of {?x}, which taints it: {?x} is bad")
(rule kill  () :match (bad ?x) :assert (false) :why "{?x} is bad, which cannot be")
(solve :no-hypothesis (bad))
(query :goal (thing ?x))
